When the application context shuts down — triggered by `context.close()`, a JVM shutdown hook, or a SIGTERM signal — Spring calls destruction callbacks in reverse order:

1. `@PreDestroy` — the annotation-based cleanup
2. `DisposableBean.destroy()` — the interface-based cleanup
3. Custom `destroy-method` — declared in `@Bean(destroyMethod = "close")`

This is where you close connections, flush buffers, deregister from service registries, and complete in-flight operations. The critical detail is that **destruction callbacks only fire for singleton beans**. Prototype beans are created and handed off — Spring doesn't track them and never calls their destruction methods. If your prototype bean opens a database connection, you must close it manually.

For graceful shutdown in production, Spring Boot 2.3+ supports a graceful shutdown period. When the application receives SIGTERM, it stops accepting new requests, waits for in-flight requests to complete (up to a configurable timeout), then runs destruction callbacks. Without graceful shutdown, destruction callbacks fire immediately — potentially interrupting active requests.

```java
@PreDestroy
public void shutdown() {
    log.info("Flushing pending metrics...");
    metricsBuffer.flush();
    log.info("Deregistering from service registry...");
    serviceRegistry.deregister(instanceId);
}
```

The `@PreDestroy` method runs in a single thread — if it blocks indefinitely (waiting for a connection that's already dead), it delays the entire shutdown process. Always add timeouts to cleanup operations.

The rule is simple: pure Java initialization (assigning fields, creating data structures) goes in the constructor. Anything that depends on injected dependencies or Spring infrastructure goes in `@PostConstruct`.

```java
@Service
public class PriceCache {
    private final PriceRepository priceRepository;
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public PriceCache(PriceRepository priceRepository) {
        this.priceRepository = priceRepository;
        // DON'T load prices here — this is the constructor
        // priceRepository is available (constructor injection), but
        // @Transactional proxy wrapping hasn't happened yet
    }

    @PostConstruct
    public void warmCache() {
        // Safe: all injection complete, proxies created
        List<Price> prices = priceRepository.findAllActive();
        prices.forEach(p -> cache.put(p.getSku(), p.getAmount()));
        log.info("Price cache warmed with {} entries", cache.size());
    }

    @PreDestroy
    public void logShutdown() {
        log.info("Price cache shutting down with {} entries", cache.size());
    }
}
```

The `warmCache()` method can safely call `priceRepository.findAllActive()` because, by the time `@PostConstruct` runs, the repository is injected and any `@Transactional` proxy wrapping is complete. Calling the same method in the constructor would work with constructor injection, but it bypasses transactional behavior because the proxy hasn't been applied yet.

One practical constraint: `@PostConstruct` methods must be `void`, take no arguments, and not throw checked exceptions. If your initialization can fail (database unreachable, config invalid), throw a `RuntimeException` — this prevents the bean from being created and, for singletons, stops the application from starting with a broken bean.

In application code, `@PostConstruct` is always sufficient. But consider a reusable connection pool that needs internal setup before user-customizable configuration runs. The `InitializingBean` interface guarantees the pool's core initialization completes first.

```java
public class ManagedConnectionPool implements InitializingBean, DisposableBean {
    private int minConnections = 5;
    private int maxConnections = 20;
    private ConnectionFactory factory;
    private List<Connection> pool;

    @Override
    public void afterPropertiesSet() {
        if (factory == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "ConnectionFactory must be configured before initialization");
        }
        pool = new ArrayList<>(minConnections);
        for (int i = 0; i < minConnections; i++) {
            pool.add(factory.createConnection());
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void destroy() {
        pool.forEach(Connection::close);
    }
}
```

The `afterPropertiesSet()` method validates configuration and creates the initial connection pool. If this were a `@PostConstruct` method and the consuming application also used `@PostConstruct` for custom setup, the execution order between them would be undefined. With `InitializingBean`, the pool is guaranteed to be initialized before any `@Bean(initMethod = ...)` runs — giving the consuming application's init-method a fully ready pool to work with.

For third-party classes that you can't modify — a database driver, a cache client, a legacy library — use `@Bean(initMethod = "init", destroyMethod = "close")` to hook into whatever setup and cleanup methods the class already provides. This is the least invasive option because it requires no modifications to the class itself.

The pattern where Aware interfaces genuinely help is when you need to resolve beans **dynamically** — where the type or name comes from configuration, user input, or runtime state.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentProcessorFactory implements ApplicationContextAware {
    private ApplicationContext context;

    @Override
    public void setApplicationContext(ApplicationContext context) {
        this.context = context;
    }

    public PaymentProcessor getProcessor(String method) {
        String beanName = method + "PaymentProcessor";
        if (!context.containsBean(beanName)) {
            throw new UnsupportedPaymentMethodException(method);
        }
        return context.getBean(beanName, PaymentProcessor.class);
    }
}
```

The factory resolves a bean name at runtime based on the payment method — `"stripe"` becomes `"stripePaymentProcessor"`. This can't be done with static injection because the bean name isn't known until a request arrives.

However, even this use case has a simpler alternative in modern Spring: inject a `Map<String, PaymentProcessor>`, and Spring automatically populates it with all `PaymentProcessor` beans keyed by their bean name.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentProcessorFactory {
    private final Map<String, PaymentProcessor> processors;

    public PaymentProcessorFactory(Map<String, PaymentProcessor> processors) {
        this.processors = processors;
    }

    public PaymentProcessor getProcessor(String method) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(processors.get(method + "PaymentProcessor"))
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UnsupportedPaymentMethodException(method));
    }
}
```

This achieves the same dynamic resolution without any Aware interface. The map injection is testable (just pass a regular `Map` in tests), visible in the constructor signature, and validated at startup. The Aware interface version requires an `ApplicationContext` mock in tests and hides the dependency from the constructor.

For `EnvironmentAware`, the same advice applies: `@Value` or `@ConfigurationProperties` is almost always better than programmatic `Environment` access. Reach for Aware interfaces only when the dependency is genuinely dynamic.

The event system has three parts: the event object (any POJO), the publisher (`ApplicationEventPublisher`), and the listener (a method annotated with `@EventListener`).

```java
public record OrderPlacedEvent(String orderId, String customerId, BigDecimal total) {}

@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;

    public OrderService(ApplicationEventPublisher events) {
        this.events = events;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepository.save(order);
        events.publishEvent(new OrderPlacedEvent(
            order.getId(), order.getCustomerId(), order.getTotal()));
    }
}

@Component
public class InventoryListener {
    @EventListener
    public void handleOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        inventoryService.reserve(event.orderId());
    }
}
```

The listener method parameter type determines which events it receives — Spring uses type matching, not naming conventions. Multiple listeners can handle the same event type. The `@EventListener` method can return a new event, creating an event chain: "order placed" → listener publishes "inventory reserved" → another listener reacts.

Because events are synchronous by default, the `handleOrderPlaced()` listener runs inside `placeOrder()`'s transaction. If inventory reservation fails and throws, the order save is rolled back. This is often the desired behavior for critical side effects, but a surprise for non-critical ones like sending a notification email.

Spring publishes built-in events during application startup and shutdown. The most useful is `ApplicationReadyEvent` — it fires after the application context is fully refreshed, all beans are created, and the application is ready to accept traffic. This is the right place for warmup logic that should run once at startup.

```java
@Component
public class StartupActions {
    @EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class)
    public void onReady() {
        log.info("Application started, warming caches...");
        cacheService.warmAll();
    }

    @EventListener(ContextClosedEvent.class)
    public void onShutdown() {
        log.info("Application shutting down, flushing buffers...");
        bufferService.flushAll();
    }
}
```

The startup event order is: `ApplicationStartingEvent` → `ApplicationEnvironmentPreparedEvent` → `ApplicationContextInitializedEvent` → `ApplicationPreparedEvent` → `ContextRefreshedEvent` → `ApplicationStartedEvent` → `ApplicationReadyEvent`. For most applications, only `ApplicationReadyEvent` and `ContextClosedEvent` matter.

`@TransactionalEventListener` is a powerful variant that defers event handling until the current transaction commits. Use it for side effects that should only happen if the transaction succeeds — sending a confirmation email after an order is confirmed, not before. Set `phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT` to fire only on success, or `AFTER_ROLLBACK` for compensation logic.

Each ordering mechanism solves a different problem. Using the wrong one creates confusion.

**`@DependsOn("beanName")`** — Forces creation order between specific beans. Use it when Bean A must be fully initialized before Bean B is created, but there's no injection dependency between them.

```java
@Component
@DependsOn("dataSourceInitializer")
public class CacheWarmer {
    @PostConstruct
    public void warm() {
        // Database is guaranteed to be populated by now
    }
}
```

**`@Order(n)` / `Ordered`** — Controls execution priority among beans of the same type. Lower numbers execute first. This does NOT control bean creation order — it controls which bean takes priority when Spring processes a collection of implementations.

```java
@Component
@Order(1)
public class SecurityFilter implements Filter { }

@Component
@Order(2)
public class LoggingFilter implements Filter { }
```

**`SmartLifecycle`** — Assigns beans to numbered phases. Phase 0 starts before phase 1, and during shutdown, higher phases stop first. Beans within the same phase start concurrently. Use this for services that need coordinated startup/shutdown — starting a message consumer only after the database connection pool is ready, or stopping the HTTP server before closing database connections.

```java
@Component
public class MessageConsumer implements SmartLifecycle {
    @Override
    public int getPhase() { return 10; }
    @Override
    public void start() { consumer.subscribe(topics); }
    @Override
    public void stop() { consumer.unsubscribe(); }
    @Override
    public boolean isRunning() { return consumer.isSubscribed(); }
}
```

`SmartLifecycle` is the most powerful option — it gives explicit phase control and participates in Spring Boot's graceful shutdown. For simple "this before that" relationships, `@DependsOn` is sufficient.

The two methods in `BeanPostProcessor` run at different points in the bean lifecycle, and the distinction determines which features each hook can implement.

**`postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`** runs after dependency injection but before `@PostConstruct`. This is where Spring processes annotations that configure the bean — `@Autowired`, `@Value`, `@Resource`. The bean receives its dependencies in this phase.

**`postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName)`** runs after `@PostConstruct` and all initialization callbacks. This is where Spring creates **proxies**. The method can return a different object than the one it received — and that replacement becomes the bean that other components inject. `@Transactional`, `@Async`, `@Cacheable`, and AOP aspects all create proxies in this hook.

```java
@Component
public class TimingBeanPostProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    @Override
    public Object postProcessBeforeInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        // Runs before @PostConstruct
        return bean;  // Return the same bean, or a modified/replaced one
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        // Runs after @PostConstruct — can wrap in proxy
        if (bean.getClass().isAnnotationPresent(Timed.class)) {
            return createTimingProxy(bean);
        }
        return bean;
    }
}
```

Returning a different object from `postProcessAfterInitialization()` is how proxy-based features work. The original `OrderService` instance is created, initialized, then replaced by a proxy that adds transactional behavior around each method call. Every other bean that injects `OrderService` gets the proxy, not the original.

The most common custom `BeanPostProcessor` pattern is annotation processing — scanning beans for a custom annotation and performing some action. This example processes a `@Metrics` annotation to automatically register method execution metrics.

```java
@Component
public class MetricsAnnotationProcessor implements BeanPostProcessor {
    private final MeterRegistry registry;

    public MetricsAnnotationProcessor(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    @Override
    public Object postProcessAfterInitialization(Object bean, String beanName) {
        Class<?> targetClass = AopUtils.getTargetClass(bean);
        if (targetClass.isAnnotationPresent(Metrics.class)) {
            ProxyFactory proxyFactory = new ProxyFactory(bean);
            proxyFactory.addAdvice((MethodInterceptor) invocation -> {
                Timer.Sample sample = Timer.start(registry);
                try {
                    return invocation.proceed();
                } finally {
                    sample.stop(Timer.builder("method.execution")
                        .tag("class", targetClass.getSimpleName())
                        .tag("method", invocation.getMethod().getName())
                        .register(registry));
                }
            });
            return proxyFactory.getProxy();
        }
        return bean;
    }
}
```

`AopUtils.getTargetClass(bean)` is critical — it returns the actual class even if the bean is already wrapped in a proxy. Without it, you'd inspect the proxy class, which doesn't have your `@Metrics` annotation, and the processor would silently skip the bean.

The `postProcessAfterInitialization` hook is used because the proxy must wrap the fully initialized bean — including any other proxies already applied (like `@Transactional`). Using the "before" hook would create a proxy around a partially initialized bean, and later proxies would wrap your proxy, creating a fragile nesting order.

The broken version injects the prototype directly. The fixed version uses `ObjectProvider` to defer instantiation to the point of use.

**Broken — prototype injected once, shared forever:**
```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ReportContext context;  // Injected once at startup

    public ReportService(ReportContext context) {
        this.context = context;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        context.reset();  // Attempt to reuse — NOT thread-safe
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

Calling `reset()` doesn't make this safe. If two threads call `generate()` concurrently, they call `reset()` and `loadData()` on the same `context` object, interleaving their data.

**Fixed — fresh prototype on every call:**
```java
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider;

    public ReportService(ObjectProvider<ReportContext> contextProvider) {
        this.contextProvider = contextProvider;
    }

    public Report generate(ReportRequest request) {
        ReportContext context = contextProvider.getObject();  // New instance
        context.loadData(request);
        return context.build();
    }
}
```

Each call to `getObject()` creates a fresh `ReportContext`. No sharing, no reset, no thread-safety concerns. The key insight is that `ObjectProvider` wraps the bean factory's prototype creation — it's functionally equivalent to `applicationContext.getBean(ReportContext.class)` but without coupling your code to the ApplicationContext API.

Remember that Spring does **not** manage prototype bean destruction. The prototype instance you get from `getObject()` is yours to manage. If it holds resources (connections, file handles), you must close them explicitly — `@PreDestroy` won't be called on prototype beans.

The default Spring Boot shutdown is immediate — `@PreDestroy` fires while requests might still be in progress. In production, you need two things: graceful request draining and time-bounded cleanup.

```yaml
# application.yml
server:
  shutdown: graceful
spring:
  lifecycle:
    timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 20s
```

With `shutdown: graceful`, Spring Boot's embedded server stops accepting new connections on SIGTERM, then waits up to `timeout-per-shutdown-phase` for active requests to complete. After draining or timeout, `@PreDestroy` methods run.

```java
@Service
public class EventPublisher {
    private final BlockingQueue<Event> buffer = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();
    private final ScheduledExecutorService scheduler;

    @PreDestroy
    public void shutdown() {
        log.info("Flushing {} pending events...", buffer.size());
        List<Event> remaining = new ArrayList<>();
        buffer.drainTo(remaining);
        try {
            eventSink.sendBatch(remaining);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("Failed to flush events during shutdown", e);
        }
        scheduler.shutdown();
        try {
            if (!scheduler.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                scheduler.shutdownNow();
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            scheduler.shutdownNow();
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}
```

The shutdown logic has three important practices: it drains the buffer first (saving data), it wraps the flush in try-catch (so one failure doesn't prevent other beans from shutting down), and it uses `awaitTermination` with a timeout (so a stuck thread doesn't block the entire shutdown). The total time spent in `@PreDestroy` methods across all beans must fit within Kubernetes' termination grace period minus the graceful drain time — plan your timeouts accordingly.
